WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.183
Northbank answers a quote in 40 milliseconds, most of the time.

2
00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:12.804
But one call in twenty takes 600, a garbage collection pause or a slow replica.

3
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:19.824
So the p99, the time 99 of 100 requests beat, is 600 milliseconds.

4
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:21.575
A retry won't help.

5
00:00:21.575 --> 00:00:22.791
Nothing failed.

6
00:00:22.791 --> 00:00:24.576
The call is just slow.

7
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.877
Watch one request.

8
00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:30.896
By 80 milliseconds, 19 calls in 20 have answered.

9
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.499
A fast call answers at 40, and nothing else happens.

10
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.401
Now the first call is a slow one.

11
00:00:37.401 --> 00:00:39.632
At 80, it still hasn't answered.

12
00:00:39.632 --> 00:00:42.629
So send one more identical call: the hedge.

13
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.952
Which answer comes first, and when?

14
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:49.858
The hedge, at 120: 80 of waiting, plus 40.

15
00:00:49.858 --> 00:00:52.603
Take it, and cancel the slow call.

16
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:57.934
Skip the cancel, and in Kotlin coroutineScope waits for every child.

17
00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:01.040
The request ends with the slow call, at 600.

18
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.621
Back to all twenty calls.

19
00:01:04.621 --> 00:01:08.625
Only the slow one got a hedge, and it now ends at 120.

20
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:15.814
In the lesson's demo, 200 quotes: the p99 fell from 600 to 120 milliseconds.

21
00:01:15.814 --> 00:01:18.937
The median, the p50, stayed at 40.

22
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:22.184
The cost: 210 calls instead of 200.

23
00:01:22.184 --> 00:01:26.668
Hedging at the 95th percentile adds about 5 percent.

24
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.970
A hedge is a duplicate request.

25
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.661
Northbank's prices are indicative, so asking twice changes nothing.

26
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:36.534
Fjord's quotes are firm.

27
00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:40.771
Each call reserves liquidity, so a hedge would reserve twice.

28
00:01:40.771 --> 00:01:42.716
Fjord gets exactly one call.

29
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.459
And the hedge takes its own permit from Northbank's bulkhead.

30
00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:51.544
Under full load it waits for one, so it can't add to the queue that made Northbank slow.

31
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:58.448
Here it is in Kotlin, the lab's language, with the Java equivalent in Reactor below it.

32
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:01.426
The rule.

33
00:02:01.426 --> 00:02:10.145
Hedge only calls that are safe to repeat, only where the tail hurts, after about the p95 latency, and cancel the loser.

34
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.453
Check yourself.

35
00:02:11.453 --> 00:02:16.307
You move the hedge to 30 milliseconds, below Northbank's normal 40.

36
00:02:16.307 --> 00:02:18.771
How many calls do 200 quotes make?

37
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:21.804
400.

38
00:02:21.804 --> 00:02:27.807
At 30, every call is still running, so every request is hedged: twice the load.
